[04:26] CANDIDATE: We split the portal into a shell and remotes owned by different teams, loaded with Module Federation so each could deploy independently. The hardest part was shared dependencies, because two remotes pulling different React versions broke hooks, so we pinned singletons for React and the router.
[04:57] INTERVIEWER: That's the crux — shared singletons. Say a shared library like the router needs a version bump; how do you roll that out without breaking one of the remotes that hasn't updated yet?
    [item=deep_dive; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=882ms wall=1.1s; activity=## Activity (what they did while speaking, and since) | While speaking (17 s): | - -2.0–17.3s pointer on “buy-sell transactions as he likes” in the problem (19.3 s) | Since they last spoke (20 s): no typing or drawing.]
<<READY 04:57>>
[04:57] CANDIDATE: We'd use a compatible version range for the shared router so remotes on the older patch still resolve to the same singleton, and for a breaking bump we'd ship a shim in the shell and migrate remotes team by team over a release or two.
[05:27] INTERVIEWER: That's a sensible migration path. Two more angles. First, if one remote fails to load or throws at runtime, how does the shell keep the rest of the portal usable?
